{"candidates":[{"claim":"DeepReason's per-target warrants (SRC_008) create non-linear coverage debt that prevents criticism from retiring rival positions as they grow from 18 to 60; transitioning to ensemble-based class criticism could retire clusters of rivals simultaneously and reduce coverage debt.","counterconditions":["If the batch criticism mechanism (SRC_008) can be trivially scaled to handle 60+ rival positions without structural changes, coverage debt is not the bottleneck.","If the 60 rival positions do not actually share common structural flaws, class-level (ensemble) criticism will be too blunt to retire them and may lead to false dismissals.","If per-target warrants are computationally cheap and the debt is purely an attention-weighting issue (SRC_001), ensemble criticism would not resolve the underlying attention saturation."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Currently, each accepted rival requires an isolated warrant, meaning the criticism effort scales poorly. The system lacks a mechanism to group similar rivals and criticize their shared structural flaws, so as rivals increase, the required warrants outpace batch criticism capacity. A sandboxed Python simulation (simulation_mode sandboxed_python_v1) could model coverage debt growth, comparing O(N^2) coverage debt for per-target warrants against an O(N log N) clustering approach for ensemble-based criticism. A directed research fetch to https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ensemble_learning would provide theoretical grounding for clustering and retiring entire classes of rival positions based on shared vulnerabilities.","optional_refs":["74fde5320d6c79520ea82d22a3e0c25a5ecc8e0cb50c3197058c5a3b0ecd2845","8e1eb4f6e21b65191f84fa3b5d2818d434ad498060eb9e4b99331a6e86cf26d6","e6e6345c01447a7cb9547070c63ca29abb8f9c547a6f2d56fc2681eae97de249","db03ce852ae608b96a7e21bc1f75c0bb73c27c7c51acbdcd1879c214233a4043"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["74fde5320d6c79520ea82d22a3e0c25a5ecc8e0cb50c3197058c5a3b0ecd2845","8